I claim that the answer is all even  $n.$  

Suppose that  $n$  is odd. Firstly, note that  $n$  must be the last coin encountered because if one goes  $n$  steps clockwise from coin  $n,$  they will get back to coin  $n,$  not allowing any other coins to be encountered. Therefore, coins  $1, \ldots, n-1$  must all be encountered before coin  $n$  is encountered, and exactly once each or else a cycle will form. However, since
\begin{align*}
    1+\ldots+(n-1) &= \frac{n(n-1)}{2} 
    &\equiv 0 \pmod{n}
\end{align*}
for all odd  $n,$  it follows that after encountering coins  $1, \ldots, n-1$  in some order, coin  $1$  must be the next coin, contradiction. 

Now, if  $n$  is even, then the coins can be ordered like so in clockwise order:   $$ 1, 2n-2, 2n-4, \ldots, 2, 2n, 2n-1, 2n-3, \ldots, 3. $$   One can verify that all coins are indeed encountered for this configuration, concluding the proof.  $\square$

