Here's a trigonometric solution. Let  $P$  be a point inside  $\triangle ABC$  such that  $PB=PC$  and  $MP=MB$  (so  $\triangle PBC$  is right-isosceles). 
I claim that  $\angle XPY = 90^\circ + A$ . We need to show  $\angle BXP + \angle CYP = 270^\circ $ . 
Firstly, from power of a point we have  $BX=\frac{a^2}{2c}$ . Moreover  $BP=\frac{a}{\sqrt{2}}$  and  $\angle XBP = B-45^\circ$ .
From the cosine rule,  $$ XP^2 = \frac{a^2}{4c^2} + \frac{a^2}{2} - \frac{a^3}{\sqrt2 c} \cos (B-45^\circ) $$  Now we compute
\begin{align*}
\sin^2 \angle BXP &= PB^2 \cdot \frac{\sin^2(B-45^\circ)}{XP^2}  
&= \frac{a^2}{2} \left( \frac{\sin^2(B-45^\circ)}{\frac{a^2}{4c^2} + \frac{a^2}{2} - \frac{a^3}{\sqrt2 c} \cdot \cos (B-45^\circ)} \right) 
&= \frac{\sin ^2(B-45^\circ)}{\frac{1}{2c^2} + \frac{2c^2}{2c^2} - \frac{a\sqrt{2}}{c} (\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \cdot (\sin B + \cos B))}
&= \frac{\frac{1}{2} - \sin B \cos B}{\frac{a^2 + 2c^2 - 2ac \cos B - 2ac \sin B}{2c^2}} 
&= \frac{c^2 - 2c^2 \sin B \cos B}{a^2 + 2c^2 - (a^2 + c^2 - b^2) - 4 \Delta} 
&= \frac{c^2 - 4\Delta \cdot \frac{c \cos B}{a}}{b^2 + c^2 - 4\Delta}
\end{align*}

By symmetry,   $$  \sin^2 \angle CYP = \frac{b^2 - 4\Delta \cdot \frac{b \cos C}{a}}{b^2+c^2-4\Delta} $$  Since  $b \cos C + c \cos B = a$  we have  $\sin^2 \angle BXP + \sin^2 \angle CYP = 1$  which gives  $\angle BXP + \angle CYP = 270^\circ$ .
The pentagon  $BXPYC$  gives  $\angle XPY = 540^\circ - 270^\circ - B - C = 90^\circ + \angle A$ .  $\quad \square$ Now we have  $\angle XPY + \angle XBP + \angle YCP = 180^\circ$  which is enough to imply the circumcircles of  $\triangle BYP$  and  $\triangle CYP$  are tangent at  $P$ .  $\quad \blacksquare$

Let us replace  $k$  by  $n$  in the statement of the problem.

We will induct on  $n$ . The base case  $n=1$  is trivial. Now suppose we have  $n+1$  variables. Apply induction hypothesis on  $x_1,x_3,\ldots,x_{n+1}$ . It suffices to prove that  $$ \frac{F_{n+1}(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_{n+1})}{F_1(x_1)}\ge\prod_{i=2}^{n+1}\frac{F_i(x_i,x_i,\ldots,x_i)}{F_{i-1}(x_i,x_i,\ldots,x_i)}. $$  **Claim.** For each  $i=2,\ldots,n$  one has  $$ \frac{F_{i}(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_i)}{F_{i-1}(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_{i-1})}\ge\frac{F_i(x_i,x_i,\ldots,x_i)}{F_{i-1}(x_i,x_i,\ldots,x_i)}. $$  *Proof.* It follows from
\begin{align*}
\frac{F_{i}(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_i)}{F_{i-1}(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_{i-1})}&=1+\frac{\prod_{j=1}^ix_j}{F_{i-1}(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_{i-1})}
&=1+\frac{x_i}{F_{i-1}\left(\frac{1}{x_{i-1}},\frac{1}{x_{i-2}},\ldots,\frac{1}{x_1}\right)}
&\ge 1+\frac{x_i}{F_{i-1}\left(\frac{1}{x_i},\frac{1}{x_i},\ldots,\frac{1}{x_i}\right)}
&=1+\frac{x_i^i}{F_{i-1}(x_i,x_i,\ldots,x_i)}
&=\frac{F_i(x_i,x_i,\ldots,x_i)}{F_{i-1}(x_i,x_i,\ldots,x_i)}.\square
\end{align*}
Multiplying the inequalities in the claim ends the proof.

