<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $\epsilon=e^{\frac{2i\pi}{3}}$ . Let  $(i,j)$  denote the cell in the  $i$ -th column and  $j$ -th row. Let  $(x,y)$  be the cell on which we have  $1 \times 1$  tile. Put the number  $\epsilon^{mi+nj}$  in the cell  $(i,j)$  where  $m,n$  are constants which are  $\leq 2$ . Note that  $1+\epsilon+\epsilon^2=0$  and  $1+\epsilon^2+\epsilon^{4}=0$ . We claim that sum of numbers associated to any rectangle is  $0$ . We prove this for  $1 \times 3$  rectangles, the case for  $3 \times 1$  rectangles can be done in a similar way. Now we must prove that  $\epsilon^{mi+nj}+\epsilon^{mi+n(j+1)}+\epsilon^{mi+n(j+2)}=0$ . Indeed, this is just  $\epsilon^{mi} \cdot \epsilon^{nj} (1+\epsilon^n+\epsilon^{2n})$  which is  $0$  for  $n=1,2,$  as claimed. Due to this, the sum of numbers on whole board must be equal to the number associated to the cell  $(x,y)$  which is  $\epsilon^{mx+ny}$ . Therefore,   $$ \epsilon^{mx+ny} = \sum_{\overset{1 \leq i \leq 7}{1\leq j \leq 7}} \epsilon^{mi+nj} = \left(\sum_{1\leq i \leq 7} \epsilon^{mi}\right) \left(\sum_{1\leq i \leq 7} \epsilon^{mi}\right) = \epsilon^m\cdot \left(\frac{\epsilon^{7m}-1}{\epsilon^m-1}\right) \cdot \epsilon^n \cdot \left(\frac{\epsilon^{7n}-1}{\epsilon^n-1}\right) = \epsilon^{m+n} $$   implying  $m+n \equiv mx+ny \pmod{3}$ . Substituting values for  $m,n$  (both are  $\leq 2$ ) and doing some case-work, we see that  $x \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$  and  $y \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ . Obviously such points either lie on boundary or on the central square. This finishes the proof.</details>
